window.__saMergeCourse({id:'class10-math', title:'Mathematics', urdu:'ریاضی', class:'Class 10',
desc:'Quadratic equations, variations, sets, statistics, trigonometry and circle geometry — full Class 10 Maths.',
icon:'📐', chapters:[
{id:'c10-mat-ch1', title:'Quadratic Equations', urdu:'درجہ دوم کی مساواتیں', comingSoon:false, lessons:[
{id:'c10-mat-ch1-l1', title:'Standard Form and Solution by Factorization', html:`<h3>Quadratic equation</h3><p>An equation of the form:</p><blockquote>ax² + bx + c = 0, &nbsp; a ≠ 0</blockquote><p>The highest power is 2, so it has <strong>at most two roots</strong> (solutions).</p><h3>Solution by factorization</h3><p>Write the equation as a product of linear factors, then use: if AB = 0, then A = 0 or B = 0.</p><h4>Worked example</h4><p>x² − 5x + 6 = 0 → (x − 2)(x − 3) = 0 → x = <strong>2 or 3</strong>.</p><p>Always bring everything to one side first: 2x² = 8 → x² − 4 = 0 → (x−2)(x+2) = 0 → x = ±2.</p><p><strong>Check:</strong> substitute each root back — (2)² − 5(2) + 6 = 0 ✓.</p>`},
{id:'c10-mat-ch1-l2', title:'Completing the Square', html:`<h3>Method</h3><p>Rewrite ax² + bx + c = 0 as a <strong>perfect square</strong>:</p><ol><li>Divide by a so the x² coefficient is 1.</li><li>Move the constant to the right.</li><li>Add <strong>(b/2)²</strong> to both sides.</li><li>Write the left as (x + b/2)² and take square roots.</li></ol><h4>Worked example</h4><p>x² + 6x + 5 = 0 → x² + 6x = −5 → add (6/2)² = 9: x² + 6x + 9 = 4 → (x + 3)² = 4 → x + 3 = ±2 → x = <strong>−1 or −5</strong>.</p><p>This method works even when factorization fails — it is the <strong>proof route</strong> to the quadratic formula.</p>`},
{id:'c10-mat-ch1-l3', title:'The Quadratic Formula', html:`<h3>Formula</h3><p>For ax² + bx + c = 0:</p><blockquote>x = (−b ± √(b² − 4ac)) / 2a</blockquote><h4>Worked example</h4><p>2x² + 5x − 3 = 0: a = 2, b = 5, c = −3.</p><p>x = (−5 ± √(25 − 4(2)(−3))) / 4 = (−5 ± √49) / 4 = (−5 ± 7)/4 → x = <strong>1/2 or −3</strong>.</p><h4>Another example</h4><p>x² − 4x + 1 = 0: x = (4 ± √(16 − 4))/2 = (4 ± √12)/2 = (4 ± 2√3)/2 = <strong>2 ± √3</strong>.</p><p><strong>Exam tip:</strong> Copy a, b, c carefully — a sign error in c is the most common lost mark. Simplify surds in the final answer.</p>`},
{id:'c10-mat-ch1-l4', title:'Word Problems with Quadratics', html:`<h3>Strategy</h3><ol><li>Let the unknown be x (define it clearly).</li><li>Translate the words into an equation.</li><li>Solve and <strong>reject impossible answers</strong> (negative lengths, etc.).</li></ol><h4>Worked example</h4><p><em>The product of two consecutive even numbers is 168. Find them.</em></p><p>Let numbers be x and x + 2: x(x + 2) = 168 → x² + 2x − 168 = 0 → (x + 14)(x − 12) = 0 → x = 12 (reject −14 if "natural numbers" asked... here x = 12 or −14, both valid pairs: <strong>12 &amp; 14</strong>, or −14 &amp; −12).</p><h4>Area problem</h4><p><em>A rectangle's length exceeds its width by 3 cm; area 70 cm².</em> x(x+3) = 70 → x² + 3x − 70 = 0 → (x+10)(x−7) = 0 → width <strong>7 cm</strong>, length <strong>10 cm</strong> (reject −10).</p>`}
], quiz:[
{q:'Which is a quadratic equation?', options:['2x + 3 = 0','x² − 5x + 6 = 0','x³ − 1 = 0','1/x + 2 = 0'], answer:1, explain:'Highest power 2 with x² term present.'},
{q:'The roots of x² − 5x + 6 = 0 are:', options:['1, 6','2, 3','−2, −3','5, 6'], answer:1, explain:'(x−2)(x−3) = 0 gives x = 2 or 3.'},
{q:'To complete the square in x² + 8x, add:', options:['8','16','64','4'], answer:1, explain:'Add (8/2)² = 16.'},
{q:'Using the quadratic formula on x² − 4x + 1 = 0 gives:', options:['2 ± √3','4 ± √3','−2 ± √3','1 ± √3'], answer:0, explain:'x = (4 ± √12)/2 = 2 ± √3.'},
{q:'For 3x² + 2x − 1 = 0, the discriminant b² − 4ac equals:', options:['16','−8','4','10'], answer:0, explain:'4 − 4(3)(−1) = 4 + 12 = 16.'},
{q:'The roots of x² − 9 = 0 are:', options:['3 only','−3 only','±3','±9'], answer:2, explain:'(x−3)(x+3) = 0, so x = ±3.'},
{q:'A quadratic equation has at most ___ roots:', options:['1','2','3','4'], answer:1, explain:'Degree 2 means at most two roots.'},
{q:'If x = 2 is a root of x² + kx − 6 = 0, k equals:', options:['1','−1','2','−2'], answer:0, explain:'Substituting: 4 + 2k − 6 = 0 → 2k = 2 → k = 1.'},
{q:'The solution set of 2x² = 8 is:', options:['{2}','{−2}','{±2}','{4}'], answer:2, explain:'x² = 4, so x = ±2.'},
{q:'In ax² + bx + c = 0, the formula requires:', options:['a = 0','a ≠ 0','b = 0','c = 0'], answer:1, explain:'If a = 0 it is not quadratic.'}
]}]
});
